1
00:00:04,104 --> 00:00:04,838
Today.

2
00:00:04,838 --> 00:00:07,640
NASA's James Webb Space Telescope amazes

3
00:00:07,640 --> 00:00:10,643
us with its new views of the universe,

4
00:00:12,078 --> 00:00:15,415
but its images
build upon 40 years of innovation

5
00:00:15,415 --> 00:00:18,418
in infrared space telescopes.

6
00:00:22,622 --> 00:00:25,392
The universe is full of infrared light

7
00:00:25,392 --> 00:00:28,695
at wavelengths much longer
than what human eyes can see.

8
00:00:30,764 --> 00:00:34,300
We need space
telescopes equipped with special detectors

9
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:37,303
to reveal these hidden wonders.

10
00:00:38,338 --> 00:00:39,205
The first

11
00:00:39,205 --> 00:00:42,609
was the Infrared Astronomical Satellite,
or IRAS,

12
00:00:42,675 --> 00:00:44,744
launched in 1983.

13
00:00:44,744 --> 00:00:48,581
IRAS measured
the infrared glow of hundreds

14
00:00:48,581 --> 00:00:51,584
of thousands of stars and galaxies.

15
00:00:53,820 --> 00:00:55,522
In 2003,

16
00:00:55,522 --> 00:00:59,526
the Spitzer Space Telescope began
a new era of infrared astronomy.

17
00:01:01,227 --> 00:01:04,431
Spitzer revealed details of galaxies
and nebulas

18
00:01:04,631 --> 00:01:07,634
never seen before.

19
00:01:08,701 --> 00:01:10,970
Now Webb takes us even deeper

20
00:01:10,970 --> 00:01:13,973
into the infrared universe.

21
00:01:20,313 --> 00:01:22,082
We look forward to the wonders yet

22
00:01:22,082 --> 00:01:25,752
to be revealed by Webb
and future infrared telescopes.
